Because {ganai abu jetnu ge lo du'u abu nibli by kei ku jetnu gi by jetnu}.
(In case my lojban is wrong) If A is true and {A nibli B} is true, then B is
true.

However, (taking {lo nibyti'i} to mean evidence, which I still don't like)
{naku zo'u ganai abu jetnu ge lo du'u abu ba'e nibyti'i by kei ku jetnu gi
by jetnu}. It is not true that if A is true and {A nibyti'i B} is true, then
B is true.

You might be eating it to be polite, or because you've been deprived of food
for the last week; it is not true that there is a logical implication, but
your eating it _is_ evidence that you like it.

On Mon, Jul 25, 2011 at 8:43 AM, tijlan <jbotijlan@gmail.com> wrote:

> On 25 July 2011 11:09, Remo Dentato <rdentato@gmail.com> wrote:
> > {lo du'u do nelci ku se nibyti'i} -> it is evident that you like it!
> >
> > {lo nu do citka ku nibyti'i lo du'u do nelci} -> Since you're eating
> > it, you surely like it!
>
> How is that different from:
>
>  nibli lo du'u do nelci
>  It logically implies that you like it.
>
>  lo nu do citka cu nibli lo du'u do nelci
>  Your eating it logically implies that you like it.
>
> mu'o mi'e tijlan
